      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s post office opened in 1873, closed in 1882, and reopened in 1883.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Livingston%2C_California",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Livingston sits just uphill from where the Southern Pacific Railroad crossed the Merced River, a key spot for early settlers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Livingston%2C_California",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The fertile sandy soil from Merced River deposits makes Livingston a top producer of sweet potatoes, supplying 90% of those grown west of the Rockies.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25,
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Livingston%2C_California",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Foster Farms, the largest poultry producer in the western U.S., and Joseph Gallo Farms, owner of the nation’s largest dairy herd, both have major operations in Livingston.",
